# Biomedical Video Truth Verification using Scientific Evidence

This project verifies biomedical claims found in short MP4 videos. It extracts speech and visible
text, turns compound statements into atomic claims, retrieves relevant PubMed abstracts, and
predicts `TRUE`, `FALSE`, or `NOT ENOUGH INFORMATION` (NEI).

The project uses open-source models only. Video processing, claim extraction, reranking, and verdict
prediction run locally. Claim searches and returned abstracts are sent through the official NCBI
PubMed API.

> Research prototype only. A verdict is not medical advice and must not be used for diagnosis or
> treatment.

## Architecture

```text
MP4 video
  +-- FFmpeg -> 16 kHz mono WAV -> local Whisper -> timestamped speech
  +-- OpenCV -> sampled frames -> local PaddleOCR/EasyOCR -> timestamped text
                                  |
                    time-align and remove duplicate OCR
                                  |
                  local DeBERTa biomedical claim detector
                                  |
             decompose compound statements into atomic claims
                                  |
        expanded multi-query PubMed search (up to 50 candidates)
                                  |
                 local SentenceTransformer reranking
                                  |
          protected biomedical-entity consistency gate
                                  |
         BiomedBERT stance for each selected evidence passage
                                  |
       qualifier / quantity / negation completeness checks
                                  |
        aggregate SUPPORTS / CONTRADICTS / NEUTRAL evidence
                                  |
              TRUE / FALSE / NEI + confidence + audit trail
                                  |
                         Streamlit dashboard
```

The atomic-claim stage matters for statements such as “bleeding gums, dry skin, and easy bruising
are signs of vitamin C deficiency, not more colds.” Each assertion is retrieved and verified
separately, so one partly relevant abstract cannot decide the entire sentence.

## Datasets now supported

Place these files in `data/`:

| Dataset | Expected file | How it is used |
|---|---|---|
| HealthFC | `HealthFC.csv` | Three-way claim/evidence verification (`TRUE`, `FALSE`, `NEI`) |
| BioASQ 7B training | `BioASQ-training7b.zip` | Yes/no biomedical questions with snippet evidence |
| BioASQ 7B golden test | `Task7BGoldenEnriched.zip` | Held-out BioASQ evaluation records |
| SciFact | `data.tar.gz` | Scientific claim support, contradiction, and conservative NEI examples |

`bigbio.py`, `bioasq.py`, `scifact.py`, and `scinfo.json` are loader/metadata files; they do not
replace the archives above. The current project has validated and prepared all three datasets.

The preparation step deliberately:

- excludes HealthFC explanations to prevent verdict leakage;
- includes only BioASQ yes/no records;
- merges duplicate BioASQ questions and excludes a conflicting duplicate-label pair;
- excludes the unlabeled SciFact public test split;
- removes exact SciFact claims repeated in train and development from train;
- preserves dataset provenance, source IDs, official splits, and SHA-256 hashes; and
- keeps examples from the same source group out of different experiment splits.

Prepare normalized JSONL files:

```powershell
biofc-prepare-datasets --data-dir data --output artifacts/prepared_datasets
```

The supplied archives produce 3,371 normalized examples: 750 HealthFC, 874 BioASQ, and 1,747
SciFact. These are preparation counts, not model-performance results.

## Project structure

```text
src/biomed_video_fc/
|-- domain/models.py                 # Typed audit/result models
|-- media/processors.py              # FFmpeg and OpenCV
|-- speech/whisper_transcriber.py    # Local speech recognition
|-- ocr/readers.py                   # Local PaddleOCR/EasyOCR
|-- text/merger.py                   # Time alignment and meaning-safe OCR merging
|-- claims/extractor.py              # Local DeBERTa claim detector
|-- claims/decomposer.py             # Compound-to-atomic claim decomposition
|-- retrieval/entity_validation.py   # Wrong-micronutrient protection
|-- retrieval/pubmed_api.py          # Multi-query PubMed retrieval and reranking
|-- reasoning/constraints.py         # Qualifier, quantity, and negation checks
|-- reasoning/local_classifier.py    # Per-evidence stance and verdict aggregation
|-- training/datasets.py             # HealthFC, BioASQ, and SciFact loaders
|-- training/prepare_datasets.py     # Reproducible JSONL preparation
|-- training/augmentation.py         # Entity/negation/qualifier hard examples
|-- training/healthfc.py             # HealthFC-only training option
|-- training/multisource.py          # Multi-dataset BiomedBERT training
|-- training/challenge_evaluation.py # Fixed precision diagnostic
|-- evaluation.py                    # Label mapping and macro metrics
|-- pipeline.py                      # Fault-tolerant orchestration
|-- factory.py                       # Application component composition
+-- ui/streamlit_app.py              # Streamlit dashboard
```

## 1. Installation

Use Python 3.11 or 3.12. On Windows, the recommended setup is:

```powershell
winget install -e --id Python.Python.3.11
# Close and reopen PowerShell after installation.
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\scripts\setup_windows.ps1
```

The setup script detects a virtual environment whose base Python was removed. It preserves that
folder as a timestamped `.venv-backup-*` directory before creating a clean environment.

Manual setup:

```bash
python -m venv .venv
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
# macOS/Linux: source .venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -e ".[all,dev]"
```

The first installation and pretrained-model download require internet access. Runtime evidence
retrieval also needs internet access to PubMed. Use the correct GPU builds of PyTorch and
PaddlePaddle when a supported GPU is available.

## 2. Train the verifier

Recommended multi-dataset experiment:

```powershell
biofc-prepare-datasets --data-dir data --output artifacts/prepared_datasets
biofc-train-multisource --prepared-dir artifacts/prepared_datasets --output artifacts/multisource_classifier --epochs 5 --batch-size 8 --hard-negative-ratio 0.25 --seed 42
```

For a GPU-guided workflow, open
`notebooks/train_multisource_verifier_colab.ipynb` in Google Colab and run its cells in order.

The default base model is:

```text
microsoft/BiomedNLP-BiomedBERT-base-uncased-abstract-fulltext
```

Training uses class-weighted loss, validation macro-F1 for early stopping, and source-grouped
splits. It adds a capped set of training-only hard examples for wrong micronutrients, negation
flips, and unsupported “first sign” wording. The BioASQ golden split and SciFact development split
remain untouched for testing. Outputs include the best model and tokenizer, per-dataset metrics,
split example IDs, augmentation counts, epoch history, environment details, and row-level test
predictions under `artifacts/multisource_classifier/`.

No new accuracy or F1 score is claimed until that training command completes. Dataset preparation
alone is not model evaluation.

To use the newly trained checkpoint, set `classifier.model_dir` in `config/default.toml` to
`artifacts/multisource_classifier`, or set `BIOFC_CLASSIFIER_DIR` for the process. The original
HealthFC-only command remains available as `biofc-train` for reproducibility.

## 3. Configure PubMed

Enter a contact email in the Streamlit sidebar, or set it before starting the app:

```powershell
$env:BIOFC_NCBI_EMAIL = "your-real-email@example.com"
# Optional:
$env:BIOFC_NCBI_API_KEY = "your-key"
```

For each atomic claim, retrieval creates biomedical synonym variants, collects up to 50 PubMed
candidates across the queries, and reranks them locally. Keyword coverage is a permissive safety
signal, not a rule requiring the abstract to repeat the claim's negation. This allows an abstract
to contradict a claim instead of being incorrectly removed before verification. A separate entity
gate requires explicit protected concepts such as Vitamin C/ascorbic acid/scurvy to occur in the
candidate. A Vitamin A-only abstract is rejected even when its semantic similarity is high.

## 4. Run the dashboard

```powershell
streamlit run app.py
```

The result shows transcripts, atomic claims, retrieved papers, the exact passages assessed by
BiomedBERT, raw and constraint-adjusted evidence stance, matched biomedical entities, the
aggregated verdict, confidence, review warnings, timings, PubMed links, and a downloadable JSON
audit record. Related evidence remains visible when a missing qualifier changes its stance to
`NEUTRAL` and its final verdict to NEI.

## Tests

The fast tests do not load large models:

```powershell
python -m unittest discover -s tests -v
python -m compileall -q src tests app.py
ruff check src tests app.py
```

The 32 tests cover dataset loading, label mappings, atomic decomposition,
negation/dosage/time-safe text merging, synonym query expansion, PubMed parsing, protected entity
matching, wrong-micronutrient rejection, qualifier-aware stance adjustment, hard-negative
generation, per-paper stance aggregation, zero-confidence review behavior when evidence is
missing, and pipeline orchestration. Regression tests confirm that the supplied Vitamin C
pediatric abstract remains visible for bleeding gums but cannot establish the unsupported word
“first.”

After training, run the fixed diagnostic separately from the untouched test evaluation:

```powershell
biofc-evaluate-challenge --model-dir artifacts/multisource_classifier --challenge data/challenge_sets/vitamin_c_precision.jsonl --output artifacts/evaluation/vitamin_c_precision_report.json
```

This five-case challenge checks Vitamin C versus Vitamin A, negation, direct support, explicit
contradiction, and unsupported temporal qualifiers. Its score is diagnostic and must not be
reported as the main test result.

## Evaluation to report

- overall and per-dataset accuracy, macro precision/recall/F1, and confusion matrices;
- per-class F1 and NEI prediction rate;
- retrieval recall@3 and recall@50 on evidence-annotated claims;
- evidence-stance accuracy before final aggregation;
- confidence calibration and selective accuracy when review-required cases are deferred;
- Whisper word error rate, OCR character error rate, and atomic-claim extraction precision/recall;
- speech-only, OCR-only, and combined-input ablations; and
- latency and memory use on the demonstration machine.

Always keep the final test splits untouched until model and threshold choices are complete.

## Important limitations

- BioASQ yes/no answers are converted to truth labels, which is useful supervision but not
  identical to natural-language claim verification.
- SciFact is scientific-domain evidence; HealthFC is consumer health evidence; PubMed abstracts at
  runtime introduce another domain shift that must be measured.
- Automatically constructed SciFact NEI examples are conservative negatives, not human-authored
  NEI annotations.
- Rule-based atomic decomposition handles common lists and predicates but is not a full semantic
  parser. The UI exposes parent/atomic claims so mistakes can be audited.
- Protected entity matching currently covers common vitamins and minerals. Drug names and diseases
  still rely on lexical/semantic retrieval and should later use a biomedical entity linker.
- Claim-detail checks deliberately favor NEI when an abstract does not explicitly address words
  such as “first,” “only,” or an exact dosage. This improves precision but can reduce recall.
- Abstracts can omit methods and caveats. Partial, conflicting, failed, or absent evidence should
  produce NEI and require review rather than a high-confidence answer.
- A model confidence is not a clinical probability, and the system does not establish medical
  causality.
- PubMed search can miss evidence, and live results can change as PubMed is updated. Preserve
  retrieval queries and PMIDs in exported audit records.

The next milestone is to train the multi-dataset checkpoint in the supplied Colab notebook and
report the untouched test results and the separate focused challenge report without combining them.
